1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:04,288
Teacher midterm practice for C plus plus, question 9.

2
00:00:04,908 --> 00:00:08,850
Why does the element equal to zero end up in the final else branch?

3
00:00:10,550 --> 00:00:13,329
Follow the zero element through the ladder.

4
00:00:13,329 --> 00:00:18,386
First test: is zero less than minus one times ten to the minus seven? No.

5
00:00:19,006 --> 00:00:24,106
Second test: is zero greater than plus one times ten to the minus seven? No.

6
00:00:24,726 --> 00:00:29,718
Neither test is true, so the final else runs: x minus equals one hundred.

7
00:00:31,318 --> 00:00:36,122
Why test against a tiny tolerance instead of writing equals equals zero?

8
00:00:36,122 --> 00:00:39,771
Doubles carry round off: a value that should be zero can

9
00:00:39,771 --> 00:00:43,177
come out as one times ten to the minus seventeen.

10
00:00:43,177 --> 00:00:46,096
The band from minus ten to the minus seven up to

11
00:00:46,096 --> 00:00:49,076
plus ten to the minus seven catches those.

12
00:00:50,676 --> 00:00:54,265
So zero lands in the else, because it is neither below minus

13
00:00:54,265 --> 00:00:58,079
ten to the minus seven, nor above ten to the minus seven.

14
00:00:58,899 --> 00:01:02,872
Thinking equals equals zero is always reliable for doubles.

15
00:01:02,872 --> 00:01:05,942
Round off can make a zero slightly non zero.

16
00:01:06,662 --> 00:01:10,795
Exam tip: when you see tolerances like one e minus seven, the

17
00:01:10,795 --> 00:01:14,389
question is testing which band each value falls into.

18
00:01:14,389 --> 00:01:18,043
Sort every value into below, inside, or above.
